we found hamed in an abandoned building  tending to his wounds the iranian  amputee says he crossed into greece  twice  since turkey opened its borders more  than a week ago  he returned with wounds all over  they caught me and beat me while they  took me to a car i begged them and said  i'm handicapped don't beat me leave me  alone but they wouldn't listen  on the second floor a temporary family  has formed  bound by trauma and desperation  once strangers now linked by the  solidarity  of feeling ostracized i am  we might have come from third world  countries but we're not third class  citizens  we also have the right to live and have  a future for our children  afghan refugee nasir has become a kind  of father  figure he and his wife crossed into  greece  three times twice by climbing through a  fence  and once on a fisherman's boat he says  greek soldiers stripped the men to their  underwear tasered them and when they  collapsed  beat them with sticks  the handcuffed men took us to the  riverbank and kicked us into the river  they said now leave some of us managed  to cross like a fish  some like a duck two of the friends  couldn't get out of the river  we told turkish fishermen that we left  two people behind  they went to search for our friends and  we never saw them again  the greek government has not responded  to the accusations  it has stopped accepting asylum  applications for one month  and pledged to expel those entering  illegally  nasir describes what's happening to  refugees and migrants who cross into  greece  as a horror movie yet he and hamed plan  to make another attempt clinging to the  possibility  like thousands others that the next time  will have a more humane ending  natasha ghanim al jazeera edurnae on the  turkey greece border
 

